:: R5_Side_Lena_Morning [r5 sidequest]
<<set $flags.r5LenaMorning = true>>
<<moodimage "files/images/mood/r5-lena-morning-departure.png" "">>
<<objective "Watch Lena get ready.">>

Earlier that morning. 5:45 AM. Before all of it.

Lena's alarm goes off. She's doing sunrise yoga now, a new thing, three weeks old. She slides out of bed in the dark with the careful movements of someone trying not to wake a partner they're also trying not to have a conversation with.

She doesn't know I'm awake. I watch through half-closed eyes. She pulls off her sleep shirt. She's naked underneath. In the blue pre-dawn light from the window, her body is a landscape I used to know. The curve of her hip. The small of her back. The birthmark on her left shoulder blade that I kissed on our third date and she laughed and said "Nobody's ever noticed that before."

She pulls on yoga pants. A sports bra. Her bra looks like function and mine, hidden in the briefcase by the door, looks like something else entirely. She pulls her hair into a ponytail. Checks her phone. The screen lights her face and I see her smile at something and the smile is small and private and not for me.

She leaves. The door clicks. The apartment settles.

I lie in bed and think about the bra in my briefcase and the smile on her face and how both of us are leaving this bed for destinations the other one doesn't know about. We are parallel lines in a marriage that used to be a conversation. Parallel lines don't intersect. That's the definition. It's also the forecast.

<div class="continue-wrap">
    <<link "Get up. Go to the office." "R5_Opening">><</link>>
</div>

:: R5_Opening [r5 story]
<<scene "Meridian Consulting, Daniel's Office" "files/images/locations/r5-opening-package-desk.png">>
<<objective "See what's in the box.">>

I see it the second I round the corner into my office. A matte black box sitting dead center on my desk like a small expensive coffin. No label. No shipping marks. No card tucked under the lid. Just a box that costs more than its contents, which is a specific kind of message.

My briefcase hits the chair. I close the door. Not all the way, because closing your office door at 7 AM before anyone else arrives is the kind of thing people notice, and people noticing things is the entire architecture of my anxiety now.

I lift the lid.

Tissue paper. Black, not white. Folded in the precise origami style of boutiques where sales associates wear all black and never smile. Under the tissue: a bra. Nude tone, lightly padded, structured but thin enough to vanish under a dress shirt. The cups are almost flat. Almost. The suggestion of shape is there, a whisper of contour, a rumor of something that could be.

The tag says La Perla. Same brand as the panties currently pressed against my cock under my charcoal slacks.

A note, folded once, tucked into the left cup. Victoria's handwriting. I know her handwriting now. Sharp letters, zero flourish, the penmanship of a woman who signs contracts the way surgeons close incisions.

//Bathroom. Now.//

Two words. A period that hits like a gavel.

I put the lid back on. Check the hallway through the glass partition. Empty. 7:14 AM. The cleaning crew finished an hour ago. The early birds won't land until 7:30. I have a window.

I pick up the box and walk to the executive washroom on the far side of the floor. My shoes click on the marble. The panties shift against me with every step, silk on skin, a sensation I've stopped noticing except when I notice that I've stopped noticing. That's the part that gets me. Not the wearing. The forgetting.

